<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Amritsar (1809)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Amritsar of 1809 was an agreement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. It fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej River</a> as the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> and British spheres of influence. This settlement secured Ranjit Singh's eastern flank and gave him room to expand his empire westwards and northwards.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> had made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> his capital in 1799, proclaimed himself maharaja of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1801 and by 1808 controlled the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> rivers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, between the Sutlej and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, feared he would absorb them and asked the British for protection. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> at first declined, since after the Treaty of Tilsit of 1807 it feared an invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and wanted Ranjit Singh's kingdom as a buffer.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> met the Company's envoy Charles Metcalfe, and during months of talks crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> to show his hold over it and to claim the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> as his border. The Company sent troops to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>, and on 9 February 1809 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ochterlony">David Ochterlony</a> declared that further incursions south of the river would be met with force. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> made an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> unlikely, the Company offered a new treaty, and Ranjit Singh, aware of his relative military weakness, agreed to sign it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> in April 1809.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty declared perpetual friendship between the British Government and the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and the British undertook to have no concern with the Raja's territories and subjects north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>. On the left bank he was to keep no more troops than internal duties required and was not to encroach on the neighbouring chiefs. The chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a> came under British protection, free of tribute, but bound to supply British armies and join them against an enemy. On 12 June Ochterlony sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> the ratified treaty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> kept the cis-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> lands he had held before his third expedition there, but gave up Faridkot and Ambala and could make no alliance with the chiefs south of the river. The boundary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> moved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> to the Sutlej. Free to act to the north, he turned to the Gurkha occupation of the Punjab Hills and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> lasted until the British conquest of 1849.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Amritsar (1809)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Sikh Treaty of 1806 and the <b>Treaty of Amritsar</b> of 1809 fixed the Sutlej River as the boundary between Sikh and East India Company spheres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
